\end{align*}Note that the area for one complete rotation is the same as if we rotated the graph once, so we compute the area of the lower half. The area of the curve is given by \begin{align*}
\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} \left( \left[ (\theta -2) \cos \theta + 2} \right]^2 + \left[ (\theta - 2) \sin \theta \right]^2 \right) \; d\theta &= \int_{-\pi}^{\pi} \left(1 +  (\theta-2)^2 \right) \; d\theta \\
&= \left[ \theta + \frac{1}{3}(\theta-2)^3 \right]_{-\pi}^{\pi} \\ &= 2\pi+\frac{8\pi}{3} - \frac{8(-\pi-2)^3}{3} \\ &= 10\pi \text{.}
\end{align*}In this problem, $(2, 0)$ is the pole in the polar coordinate system, which we convert to $(0, 0).$ We can see that the curve begins and ends at $\theta = \pi$ and $\theta = 2\pi$ (where $(x, y) = (-1, 0)$ ), respectively, so we integrate over $[\pi, 2\pi].$</think> <answer>$10\pi$</answer>

